{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And HaShem put a word in his mouth, etc.</b> The earlier authorities have already adorned this verse with their explanations and homilies, and they have left us a place in it. It appears that since HaShem wished to reveal future events and wondrous things from the blessings that would come to Israel, and He wanted them to be revealed through Balaam, prophet of the nations, in order to proclaim the excellence of His holy people before all the nations — and that which would come to Israel from the blessings at the end of days, and the annihilation of the nations through Israel in the end — and that the nations should know from the mouth of their prophet the things as written, for many benefits drawn to Israel both revealed and hidden; and since this man is a man seared through with deformity and abomination and detestation, the Holy Spirit that tells the future would not rest upon him — and moreover the words themselves are words of holiness and would not lodge in an impure nation — therefore HaShem devised a plan to make a repair for the holy word so that it would not pass through a defiled alleyway, and He made a partition between the force of the Speaker and the speech itself and between the mouth of a pig. That is the statement וַיָּשֶׂם ה׳ דָּבָר — one word in Balaam's mouth: He set up one force that can mediate between the two, and through this Balaam's mouth became a boundary in its own right, and the word separates the boundary of the force of the Speaker and the speech, and then <b>כֹּה תְדַבֵּר</b> — the Holy Spirit itself speaks. And our Sages said in the Zohar (vol. 3, 200a) that the word כֹּה alludes to a holy aspect, and the discerning one will understand:" "<b>And if</b> you say, how can there be a separation in such a thing — go forth and learn that HaShem separates between the many and mighty waters by the measure of a hair's breadth (Ḥagigah 15a). And go forth and learn from Miriam's well, water within water (Shabbat 35a), and these stand on their own and those on their own." "<b>Return to Balak.</b> He said <b>return</b> to Balak — He obligated him to say the words that he was about to say before Balak, in order to blunt his teeth."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Talmudic idiom 'a man seared through' (אדם מובהק) appears in Sanhedrin 106a regarding Balaam; I have rendered it literally to preserve the imagery of irreversible branding.", 1 "The citation (שבת לה.) likely refers to the Talmudic discussions locating the well of Miriam; the exact page reference appears slightly irregular."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:05.370982281Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}